i}$ with $r \\gt 0$, which expression gives all complex logarithms of $z$?","options":["$r+\\mathrm{i}\\varphi$ only","$\\ln r+\\mathrm{i}(\\varphi+2\\pi k)$, where $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$","$\\ln r+\\mathrm{i}\\varphi$ only for every $z$","$\\mathrm{e}^{r+\\mathrm{i}\\varphi+2\\pi k}$, where $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0f072f01de1420e1d00127c4","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_logarithm_d6835537_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is $\\operatorname{Log}(-\\mathrm{i})$ using the principal interval $(-\\pi,\\pi]$?","options":["$-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\mathrm{i}$","$\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\mathrm{i}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\mathrm{i}$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cbf7cd117980bdad634a31bc","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_logarithm_d6835537_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For which nonzero $z,w$ does $\\operatorname{Log}(zw)\\neq\\operatorname{Log}z+\\operatorname{Log}w$, using imaginary parts in $(-\\pi,\\pi]$?","options":["$z=w=1$.","$z=1,\\ w=-1$.","$z=w=\\mathrm{i}$.","$z=w=-1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"66c624e7a1a69ab5bddc4e2e","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_logarithm_d6835537_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $r>0$, real $\\varphi$ and $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$, why do logarithms of $r\\mathrm{e}^{\\mathrm{i}\\varphi}$ include shifts $2\\pi k$ in their imaginary part?","options":["The modulus changes by $2\\pi k$.","Angles differing by $2\\pi k$ represent the same complex direction.","The real part must be an integer.","The exponential is defined only for integer inputs."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f4513fb77e711a73ba63dabf","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_logarithm_d6835537_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $z\\neq0$, why is $\\operatorname{Log}z$ single-valued while $\\log z$ is multivalued?","options":["The principal logarithm chooses one argument in a specified interval.","The principal logarithm uses one modulus, while $\\log z$ uses many moduli.","The principal logarithm keeps only the real part $\\ln|z|$ and drops the argument.","The principal logarithm is defined only for positive real numbers $z$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"66aa51ca8de2db57f3b6cbff","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_logarithm_d6835537_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $z=2\\mathrm{e}^{\\mathrm{i}\\pi/4}$. Which expression gives all logarithms of $z$?","options":["$\\ln 2+\\mathrm{i}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+2\\pi k\\right)$, $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$","$2+\\mathrm{i}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+2\\pi k\\right)$, $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$","$\\ln 2+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+2\\pi k$, $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$","$\\ln 2-\\mathrm{i}\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ only"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7986fa6e9c3506ce39b06513","time_limit":30},{"id":"repair_complex_logarithm_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For which complex numbers is $\\log z$ defined in this setting?","options":["Only for $z=0$.","Only for positive real numbers.","For nonzero complex numbers $z$.","For all complex numbers, including $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3be5206a925225255210c1ec","time_limit":25}]}}